   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT relating to extending the time within which certain elected officers may file their oaths of office",
    "basePrintNo": "S7009",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7009-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Elections",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7009\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act relating to extending the time within which certain elected\nofficers may file their oaths of office\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would allow for those duly elected officials in Oswego\nCounty in the most recent countywide election (November 2015), who\nfailed to timely file their oaths of office within the statutory\ndeadline, to properly cure that ministerial defect and serve out their\nfull two-year terms.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 The first section provides that for any duly elected\nlegislator in a county-wide race in the County of Oswego who failed to\ntimely take or file their respective oath of office, such legislators\nwill have an additional thirty day time frame from enactment of this\nlegislation to take and file their respective oaths of office. If such\nelected legislators take and file their respective oaths of office in\nthe new thirty day timeframe, they will be deemed to have been in\ntheir elected office since the commencement of their term (January 1,\n2016) and shall remain in their respective office until the conclusion\nof their term (December 31, 2017).\n\nSection 2 sets forth an immediate effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nUnder current law, once the term of a duly elected legislator begins,\nsuch elected legislator has thirty days from commencement of their\nterm to take and file an oath of office. Failure to take and file an\noath of office in the thirty day time frame disqualifies the duly\nelected legislator from serving in their office, thereby leaving the\noffice vacant. Pursuant to existing law, once a vacancy occurs, the\nserving members of the county legislature may appoint someone to fill\nthe vacancy. However, the appointment is merely temporary until an\nelection can be held in the next-occurring November to fill the\nremaining balance of that term of office.\n\nOn November 3rd, 2015, the voters of Oswego County elected County\nLegislators. On January 5th, 2016, five of the elected legislators\ntook their respective oaths of office before State Supreme Court\nJustice Norman W. Seiter, Jr. of the 5th Judicial District, and\nthereafter assumed their official duties. Unfortunately however, they\nfailed to properly file their oaths of office in the County Clerk's\nOffice within the thirty day time frame required by law. In the\nabsence of such filing, they could not legally hold the offices they\nhad just been elected to by the voters.\n\nIn a spirit of collegiality, cooperation and out of respect for the\ndecision of Oswego County voters on Election Day, the serving Oswego\nCounty Legislative Members appointed these five legislators to their\nelected positions on February 16, 2016, to serve until December 31st,\n2016, as permitted by law. As previously stated, however, without this\nlegislation these legislators would instead need to run again for\nthese elected offices this coming November in order to serve out the\nremaining balance of a two-year term. This is an unforeseen,\nunintended consequence of existing state law that should be\nimmediately corrected.\n\nIn addition, current law specifically states that an elected official\nmust \"take and file\" an oath of office within 30 days of beginning\ntheir term or it shall be deemed a refusal to serve the office. At no\ntime did the legislators take any actions that would lead one to\nbelieve they intended to refuse to serve in office. To the contrary,\non January 5, 2016, they publicly \"took\" their oaths of office clearly\nmanifesting their intent to assume the duties and responsibilities of\nthe offices they had been elected to in November.  The only obstacle\nto serving a full two-year term is their failure to \"file\" an oath of\noffice with the County Clerk in a timely manner - which was an\ninadvertent clerical defect.\n\nTo now require these legislators to run in another election for the\nremaining balance of a two-year term that seemingly has already been\ngranted by the Oswego County voters is unjust, unfair and an\nunnecessary waste of taxpayers' time and money. Most importantly, it\nhas the potential to nullify the results of a fairly held election for\nthese legislators. Enacting this legislation would uphold the decision\nof Oswego County voters, cure the ministerial defect, and preserve the\nintegrity of the electoral process.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone. This bill would result in savings to the taxpayers of Oswego\nCounty.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
